course: dictionary
lesson: 9311
title: Speech: talking and talks
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Speech has three meanings. It can mean the ability to talk, or spoken language itself: «Babies learn speech slowly.» It can also mean a talk given to an audience, like at assembly or a wedding. In grammar, direct speech is the exact words someone said, shown inside speech marks.
explain: Look at the words around speech to tell which meaning is used. «Uncle Dev shakes over his best man's speech» means a talk, because he is giving it to the guests. «Babies learn speech slowly» means the ability to talk, because babies are learning to say words.
explain: Direct speech can be split in the middle. In «"If you're ready," said Mum, "we'll go."», the second part starts with a small letter because it is still the same sentence. The speech marks show exactly where Mum's words start and stop.
fact: Speech means the ability to speak, or spoken language.
fact: A speech is a talk given to an audience, such as at assembly or a wedding.
fact: Direct speech is the exact words someone said, inside speech marks.
fact: «Speech marks» are also called quotation marks.
fact: «Speech bubbles» show the words a character says in a comic or picture.
exercise: In «"If you're ready," said Mum, "we'll go."», what are the marks round Mum's exact words called? | Brackets | Full stops | Bullet points | *Speech marks
exercise: «Uncle Dev shakes over his best man's speech.» What is a speech here? | A quiet whisper | The ability to speak | *A talk given to an audience | A type of speech bubble
exercise: «Babies learn speech slowly.» What does speech mean in this sentence? | A loud shout | A talk given at a wedding | A grammar rule about commas | *The ability to speak, or spoken language
test: What is direct speech? | *The exact words someone said, inside speech marks | Words that describe a noun | A talk given to a crowd of people | A noise made by a bird
test: Which sentence shows someone giving a speech? | Mum learned a new word at school. | Mum whispered a question. | Mum drew a picture of the hotel. | *Mum made a speech at my cousin's wedding.
exam: In «"If you're ready," said Mum, "we'll go."», why does «we'll» start with a small letter? | It is a question, so it needs a capital | It is the start of a new paragraph | *It is still the same sentence | Small letters are used for names
